Changelog: 10-10-2026 20:56:48

Release 0.1.0. Initial release

Changelog: 09-10-2026 22:59:08

README: only the app's name, what it does and its license, with a link to its chapter of the manual; the rest moved into the manual (docs/apps/system-overlay.md).

Changelog: 09-10-2026 17:51:51

Only one instance runs per user: starting the app again brings the running window to the front instead of opening a second one.

Changelog: 09-10-2026 16:37:18

New rows: GPU load (from the GPU governor's patched gpu_metrics table, in hundredths of a percent as the kernel
and MangoHud use it; only with the governor's fix-metrics), the governor's clock range (D-Bus), GPU core voltage,
VRAM and RAM used, VRM and NVMe temperatures, network download/upload and disk read/write. A Rows submenu in the
right-click menu shows or hides each row; the choice is remembered and the window resizes to fit.

Changelog: 09-10-2026 16:27:25

First version. BC-250 System Overlay shows CPU (load and clock), GPU (clock and power), the screen's refresh rate,
the fastest spinning fan and the CPU and GPU temperatures in a small window that stays on top. The window is
frameless and see-through, with rounded corners and a thin purple border; drag it with the left mouse button, and
right-click for the background opacity, Always on top and Quit. Its position and choices are remembered. It reads
/proc and /sys only, without root, and updates every second. On Wayland it runs through XWayland so it can stay on
top (--native-wayland runs it as a Wayland window instead). Built on bc250_core and the suite's shared venv;
install.sh installs it in your home folder, without sudo.
